{
  "spec": "sje/0.1.0",
  "note": "Each case gives its input as `value` (a JSON value; how this file spells it is not part of the test) or as `text` (the exact JSON text: parse it with your own JSON parser, then canonicalize the result; the spelling is the test, e.g. 10.0, -0, 1e2, an escaped letter). Valid: implementations MUST produce exactly `canonical` (as UTF-8 bytes) and `sha384`. Invalid: implementations MUST refuse, in the parser or in the canonicalizer, and produce no bytes; `refusal` holds a fragment of each reference implementation's own message (`ts`: TypeScript, where \"not JSON\" means JSON.parse rejected the text; `rs`: Rust), so a case cannot pass by being refused for some other defect. A number is read as an IEEE-754 binary64 value (round to nearest, ties to even) before the canonical rule is applied to it. Every `text` is one JSON value with no repeated member name (travelers/reject/ covers those). This file is ASCII: any other character, in a `text` or anywhere else, is written as a \\uXXXX escape of this file's own JSON, so a character that is literal in a `text` shows one backslash here and an escape inside the `text` shows two.",
